1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,363
A reset points a branch at another commit,
another saved snapshot, and the work

2
00:00:09,403 --> 00:00:14,390
after it seems to vanish. Your CTO asks
you two questions, and neither of them is

3
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:18,176
"which command do we type". The
first is: can we get it back?

4
00:00:18,216 --> 00:00:21,713
The second is: how sure are
you, and how long do we have?

5
00:00:22,053 --> 00:00:26,960
Both questions have exact answers. They
depend on three facts about the lost work.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,666
Was it ever written into the object
database, Git's store of everything it has

7
00:00:31,706 --> 00:00:35,165
saved? Does anything still name
it? Has the clock run out?

8
00:00:35,205 --> 00:00:40,233
If you can answer those three from evidence,
you can answer the CTO in a minute, and

9
00:00:40,273 --> 00:00:45,119
you can also say the harder sentence when
it's true: this can't be recovered, and

10
00:00:45,159 --> 00:00:48,618
here is exactly why. Hold on
to 18:40. We come back to it.

11
00:00:50,458 --> 00:00:54,118
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

12
00:00:54,158 --> 00:00:58,970
This video opens the recovery module. For
the next seven videos you take fifteen

13
00:00:59,010 --> 00:01:03,094
kinds of accident and bring the work
back, or prove that it is gone.

14
00:01:03,434 --> 00:01:08,364
Three earlier videos supply the foundations.
You know reachability: an object is

15
00:01:08,404 --> 00:01:13,210
kept as long as some starting point
reaches it. You know what git reset --hard

16
00:01:13,250 --> 00:01:18,366
destroys. And you know force pushes and
remote-tracking refs, your records of where

17
00:01:18,406 --> 00:01:22,901
a server's branches were. Today those
pieces become a map, four layers of

18
00:01:22,941 --> 00:01:27,560
protection, and you learn to read the
instrument you'll use in almost every

19
00:01:27,600 --> 00:01:28,864
recovery: the reflog.

20
00:01:30,704 --> 00:01:33,982
name the four layers that protect
work and what each holds;

21
00:01:34,322 --> 00:01:38,724
read a reflog entry: old ID,
new ID, selector, action;

22
00:01:39,064 --> 00:01:44,093
tell the HEAD reflog from a branch reflog
and choose the right one for an incident;

23
00:01:44,433 --> 00:01:48,416
use @{n} and time selectors, and
explain why the reflog is local;

24
00:01:48,756 --> 00:01:52,191
explain how an unreachable commit
survives a maintenance run.

25
00:01:54,031 --> 00:01:59,017
In one sentence: Git never deletes an object
that something still names, it keeps a

26
00:01:59,057 --> 00:02:03,983
private log of every name that recently
changed, and it waits before deleting what

27
00:02:04,023 --> 00:02:08,948
nothing names. Work is lost only when it
was never an object, or when all of these

28
00:02:08,988 --> 00:02:10,220
protections are gone.

29
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:15,467
Precisely. An object is kept as long as
it is reachable from a starting point.

30
00:02:15,507 --> 00:02:20,731
The manual of git gc lists the starting
points: branches and tags, the index, which

31
00:02:20,771 --> 00:02:25,996
is your proposed next commit, remote-tracking
branches, reflogs, "and anything else

32
00:02:26,036 --> 00:02:27,581
in the refs/* namespace".

33
00:02:27,921 --> 00:02:32,281
It adds one exclusion: a note attached
to an object doesn't keep it alive.

34
00:02:32,321 --> 00:02:35,610
An object that no starting
point reaches is unreachable.

35
00:02:35,650 --> 00:02:40,485
It still exists until a collection, Git's
clean-up of its store, deletes it, and a

36
00:02:40,525 --> 00:02:44,944
collection deletes only objects older
than a cut-off, two weeks by default.

37
00:02:45,284 --> 00:02:49,652
That gives four layers, and a recovery is
always a move from a lower layer back to

38
00:02:49,692 --> 00:02:50,405
the first one.

39
00:02:50,745 --> 00:02:54,251
Layer one is refs, the names
that hold an object ID.

40
00:02:54,291 --> 00:02:59,639
A branch, a tag, a remote-tracking ref,
refs/stash, HEAD or the index names the

41
00:02:59,679 --> 00:03:03,117
object. It lasts until the
ref is moved or deleted.

42
00:03:03,157 --> 00:03:06,595
You find it with git log
--all or git for-each-ref.

43
00:03:06,935 --> 00:03:10,434
Layer two is reflogs. A reflog
entry names the object.

44
00:03:10,474 --> 00:03:15,807
The manual's defaults are 90 days, or 30
days for entries off the current history.

45
00:03:15,847 --> 00:03:21,114
The lab configuration overrides both, so
that the transcripts don't depend on the

46
00:03:21,154 --> 00:03:25,177
day they were produced. You get
the details in the next video.

47
00:03:25,217 --> 00:03:28,191
You find these with git
reflog and git log -g.

48
00:03:28,531 --> 00:03:33,470
Layer three is the grace period. Nothing
names the object, but its file is younger

49
00:03:33,510 --> 00:03:38,327
than the prune cut-off. Two weeks from
the time the object was written, and then

50
00:03:38,367 --> 00:03:41,544
until the next collection
runs. The tool is git fsck.

51
00:03:41,884 --> 00:03:46,425
Layer four is other repositories: another
clone, the server, a bundle file, a

52
00:03:46,465 --> 00:03:49,696
backup. Its lifetime is
independent of this repository.

53
00:03:50,036 --> 00:03:53,745
Before the layers comes a precondition:
was the work ever an object?

54
00:03:54,085 --> 00:03:58,728
Read the middle column. Committed,
stashed, or staged with git add: an object

55
00:03:58,768 --> 00:04:03,593
exists. Saved in the working tree and
never staged, or untracked: no object, and

56
00:04:03,633 --> 00:04:05,417
nothing for Git to bring back.

57
00:04:05,757 --> 00:04:11,238
Now the reflog. In one sentence: a reflog
is a local, append-only list of the values

58
00:04:11,278 --> 00:04:16,495
a ref has had, with the time, the person
and the command behind each change, and

59
00:04:16,535 --> 00:04:19,913
every one of those old values
is a name you can use.

60
00:04:20,253 --> 00:04:25,463
Precisely. There's one reflog per ref that
has logging enabled, plus one for HEAD.

61
00:04:25,503 --> 00:04:30,073
In a repository with a working tree,
Git by default logs HEAD, branches,

62
00:04:30,113 --> 00:04:35,067
remote-tracking branches and notes refs.
Tags get no reflog unless the setting

63
00:04:35,107 --> 00:04:40,381
core.logAllRefUpdates is always. Each entry
stores the value before the change, the

64
00:04:40,421 --> 00:04:45,311
value after it, the committer identity
and time, and a message written by the

65
00:04:45,351 --> 00:04:45,823
command.

66
00:04:46,163 --> 00:04:49,700
There are two kinds, and they
answer different questions.

67
00:04:49,740 --> 00:04:54,972
The HEAD reflog records every movement of
HEAD: commits, switches, resets, each step

68
00:04:55,012 --> 00:05:00,055
of a rebase, merges, cherry-picks. It
answers "what did I do, in order", and it's

69
00:05:00,095 --> 00:05:04,008
the only log that sees detached
HEAD, when you're on no branch.

70
00:05:04,048 --> 00:05:07,773
A branch reflog records only
changes of that branch's value.

71
00:05:07,813 --> 00:05:11,288
It answers "where was this
branch before the operation".

72
00:05:11,328 --> 00:05:13,421
A whole rebase is one entry there.

73
00:05:13,761 --> 00:05:17,830
When the reflog can't help: it
records movements of refs only.

74
00:05:17,870 --> 00:05:21,343
Edits to files, staging and
unstaging leave no entry.

75
00:05:21,383 --> 00:05:25,452
And it's local. No other clone
and no server sees your reflog.

76
00:05:27,292 --> 00:05:31,604
The textbook's analogy for the layers
is a bank vault with a ledger.

77
00:05:31,644 --> 00:05:36,979
The safe-deposit boxes are objects. The
index cards at the front desk are refs: each

78
00:05:37,019 --> 00:05:42,290
card names one box. When a card is rewritten,
the clerk notes the old box number in

79
00:05:42,330 --> 00:05:47,601
a ledger, the reflog. Boxes that no card
and no ledger line mentions are emptied in

80
00:05:47,641 --> 00:05:52,465
a periodic clear-out, but only boxes
that have been untouched for two weeks.

81
00:05:52,805 --> 00:05:57,865
The analogy breaks in two places. A box
in Git also holds the numbers of older

82
00:05:57,905 --> 00:06:03,293
boxes: a commit names its parents and its
tree. So one card protects a whole chain.

83
00:06:03,333 --> 00:06:08,721
And the ledger is private to one branch
office: no other clone, and no server, sees

84
00:06:08,761 --> 00:06:09,506
your reflog.

85
00:06:09,846 --> 00:06:14,987
For the reflog alone, the textbook offers
the "undo history" of an editor, kept per

86
00:06:15,027 --> 00:06:20,167
document. That breaks in two places too. A
reflog has no undo command: it gives you

87
00:06:20,207 --> 00:06:23,351
old positions, and you
decide what to do with them.

88
00:06:23,391 --> 00:06:25,723
And it records movements of refs only.

89
00:06:27,563 --> 00:06:33,204
In layer one the branch names da62b60, and
through it every parent, tree and blob.

90
00:06:33,244 --> 00:06:38,538
In layer two a reflog entry names dbe6ec8,
a commit that was removed from its

91
00:06:38,578 --> 00:06:42,349
branch. In layer three nothing
names the object at all.

92
00:06:42,389 --> 00:06:44,565
Layer four is outside this .git.

93
00:06:44,905 --> 00:06:49,512
The ref itself holds only the new value.
Each of the two log files gains one line

94
00:06:49,552 --> 00:06:54,272
with the old value and the new one. A command
that moves the current branch appends

95
00:06:54,312 --> 00:06:55,075
to both files.

96
00:06:56,915 --> 00:07:02,658
git gc --prune=now is also 🔴 DANGEROUS: a
collection with no grace period at all.

97
00:07:02,698 --> 00:07:08,299
Before it runs, the five answers. What
it changes: it packs objects and deletes

98
00:07:08,339 --> 00:07:13,796
unreachable ones immediately. What it
can destroy: everything on layer three.

99
00:07:13,836 --> 00:07:18,936
How to preview: git prune -n lists
what an immediate prune would delete.

100
00:07:18,976 --> 00:07:24,791
How to recover: from another repository
only. When it's appropriate: you'll see in

101
00:07:24,831 --> 00:07:29,146
the last video of this module.
Here the preview is the point.

102
00:07:33,986 --> 00:07:38,434
Predict: the commit dbe6ec8 is
on no branch. Does it survive?

103
00:07:38,474 --> 00:07:40,420
Say it out loud. I'll wait.

104
00:07:45,260 --> 00:07:49,242
It survives. git prune -n prints
nothing: nothing would be deleted.

105
00:07:49,282 --> 00:07:52,783
After the collection, git
cat-file -t still answers commit.

106
00:07:52,823 --> 00:07:56,204
All twelve objects were packed,
the lost commit included.

107
00:07:56,544 --> 00:08:01,992
The reflog entry HEAD@{1} names it, so it's
on layer two, and a reflog is a starting

108
00:08:02,032 --> 00:08:03,495
point for reachability.

109
00:08:09,335 --> 00:08:14,086
Here's that history as a picture. One
branch, main, with three commits.

110
00:08:14,126 --> 00:08:18,337
Then a second branch, feature/rerank,
with one commit, 14a18d6.

111
00:08:18,677 --> 00:08:23,316
The amend replaces that commit with
f1f247e, and the old one turns dashed.

112
00:08:23,656 --> 00:08:29,460
main fast-forwards to f1f247e, and then
the reset pulls it back to da62b60.

113
00:08:29,800 --> 00:08:35,149
Try it now, on paper. Thirty seconds: write
down how many lines the HEAD reflog will

114
00:08:35,189 --> 00:08:38,870
have, counting the three commits
made before this snippet.

115
00:08:38,910 --> 00:08:40,217
Then say it out loud.

116
00:08:45,057 --> 00:08:50,676
Nine. Read it from the bottom. Each line
is "the value HEAD had after this event".

117
00:08:50,716 --> 00:08:56,267
HEAD@{0} is always the current value. The
amend at HEAD@{3} replaced 14a18d6 with

118
00:08:56,307 --> 00:09:00,339
f1f247e, and the old commit is
still listed one line below.

119
00:09:00,379 --> 00:09:05,792
The two checkout lines record branch
switches, which change HEAD and no branch.

120
00:09:06,132 --> 00:09:11,534
The reflog of main has five lines where
HEAD has nine: the switches and the work on

121
00:09:11,574 --> 00:09:16,911
the other branch are absent. git reflog
list, which needs Git 2.45 or later, names

122
00:09:16,951 --> 00:09:18,550
every ref that has a log.

123
00:09:18,890 --> 00:09:23,853
A reflog entry is addressed with a selector,
and a selector is a revision, a way of

124
00:09:23,893 --> 00:09:26,506
naming a commit, that
every command accepts.

125
00:09:26,846 --> 00:09:31,985
HEAD@{1} is the value HEAD had one change
ago. @{1} with no name is for the current

126
00:09:32,025 --> 00:09:37,102
branch, not for HEAD. @{-1} is different
in kind: it's the branch that was checked

127
00:09:37,142 --> 00:09:40,471
out before the current one,
a name and not a position.

128
00:09:40,511 --> 00:09:44,964
Quote selectors in the shell, because
braces are special to some shells.

129
00:09:45,304 --> 00:09:48,639
Time-based selectors read
the timestamps in the log.

130
00:09:48,679 --> 00:09:52,209
Predict the last command: the
log began on 7 September.

131
00:09:52,249 --> 00:09:57,402
What does "two days ago" return? Quick
quiz, three options: an error, nothing at

132
00:09:57,442 --> 00:10:00,777
all, or the oldest value
Git knows. Say it out loud.

133
00:10:05,617 --> 00:10:10,751
At 10:10:30 main was still at da62b60,
because the merge happened at 10:12.

134
00:10:10,791 --> 00:10:14,545
The last command asks for a
time before the log begins.

135
00:10:14,585 --> 00:10:20,271
Option three: Git answers with the oldest
value it knows, 538ea2f, and says so in a

136
00:10:20,311 --> 00:10:25,583
warning. A time selector is a statement
about your local log, never about the

137
00:10:25,623 --> 00:10:31,240
project. In a clone made today, main@{1.week.ago}
can't know where main was a week

138
00:10:31,280 --> 00:10:31,516
ago.

139
00:10:31,856 --> 00:10:37,250
git log -g walks a reflog instead of the
ancestry, with placeholders for the reflog

140
00:10:37,290 --> 00:10:37,709
fields.

141
00:10:38,049 --> 00:10:43,956
%gd is the selector, %gs the reflog message,
%gn the name of the person who made the

142
00:10:43,996 --> 00:10:49,691
change. --grep-reflog filters by reflog
message. The third command lists only the

143
00:10:49,731 --> 00:10:55,355
entries that created commits. That's how
you search a long log for the event you

144
00:10:55,395 --> 00:10:57,904
need, instead of counting positions.

145
00:10:58,244 --> 00:10:59,369
Last, the files.

146
00:10:59,709 --> 00:11:03,417
One event on the current branch
is two lines, one in each file.

147
00:11:03,457 --> 00:11:07,404
The first ID of a line is the old
value and the second the new one.

148
00:11:07,444 --> 00:11:12,401
That's more than git reflog prints, and it
matters: the old value of the newest line

149
00:11:12,441 --> 00:11:15,793
tells you where a ref was even
when no older line exists.

150
00:11:17,633 --> 00:11:19,281
Five mistakes to watch for.

151
00:11:19,621 --> 00:11:23,268
Looking in the reflog for an
edit that was never committed.

152
00:11:23,308 --> 00:11:28,268
Root cause: a reflog records movements of
refs only; edits to files, staging and

153
00:11:28,308 --> 00:11:29,830
unstaging leave no entry.

154
00:11:30,170 --> 00:11:34,332
Counting positions in the HEAD
reflog to find where a branch was.

155
00:11:34,372 --> 00:11:39,763
Root cause: HEAD has one line per step,
including every step of a rebase; the branch

156
00:11:39,803 --> 00:11:41,962
reflog has one line per operation.

157
00:11:42,302 --> 00:11:46,954
Writing main@{1} in an incident note. Root
cause: a selector is relative to the log

158
00:11:46,994 --> 00:11:51,421
at that moment; it means something else
after the next command and nothing on a

159
00:11:51,461 --> 00:11:53,682
colleague's machine. Record the full ID.

160
00:11:54,022 --> 00:11:58,963
Asking a teammate or the server for "your
reflog". Root cause: reflogs are never

161
00:11:59,003 --> 00:12:01,826
pushed or cloned; each
repository has its own.

162
00:12:02,166 --> 00:12:06,907
Trusting a time selector in a fresh clone.
Root cause: the log starts when the clone

163
00:12:06,947 --> 00:12:11,062
was made, and Git answers with the
oldest value it knows, with a warning.

164
00:12:12,902 --> 00:12:18,320
Back to 18:40 on release day. The first
minute of a recovery is classification, not

165
00:12:18,360 --> 00:12:18,780
typing.

166
00:12:19,120 --> 00:12:23,604
You ask the engineer what form the work
had. "Three days of commits on a feature

167
00:12:23,644 --> 00:12:28,353
branch, and I reset the branch" is layer
two: the branch reflog has the old tip, and

168
00:12:28,393 --> 00:12:32,933
with default settings the entry for a
commit off the current history lives for 30

169
00:12:32,973 --> 00:12:37,512
days. You tell the CTO: recoverable, high
confidence, weeks of margin, and nobody

170
00:12:37,552 --> 00:12:40,170
should run maintenance
in that clone meanwhile.

171
00:12:40,510 --> 00:12:45,628
A different answer changes everything. "I
had edited the files since lunch and then

172
00:12:45,668 --> 00:12:49,047
ran git restore" was never
an object. No layer applies.

173
00:12:49,087 --> 00:12:53,833
You move on to editor history and backups
at once, and you say so, instead of

174
00:12:53,873 --> 00:12:55,635
spending an hour in git fsck.

175
00:12:57,475 --> 00:13:04,199
Your turn. Do Exercise 12.1, Level 1,
"Read a reflog before you need it", in

176
00:13:04,239 --> 00:13:08,115
exercises/m11-m15-investigation-recovery.md.

177
00:13:08,455 --> 00:13:13,479
Work in labs/shell. Before you print any
reflog, predict the number of lines in the

178
00:13:13,519 --> 00:13:18,116
HEAD reflog and in the branch reflog
for the operations the exercise has you

179
00:13:18,156 --> 00:13:22,083
perform, and which lines will
appear in one and not in the other.

180
00:13:22,423 --> 00:13:28,012
The challenge is Exercise 12.4, Level 2,
command prediction, "What the reflog will

181
00:13:28,052 --> 00:13:29,590
say", in the same file.

182
00:13:31,430 --> 00:13:36,041
Pause and answer out loud. It's a short
question, and a short answer is a weak one.

183
00:13:40,881 --> 00:13:45,712
A strong answer defines the reflog by
what it records and where it lives, then

184
00:13:45,752 --> 00:13:50,958
connects it to reachability: why an entry
keeps a commit alive through a collection.

185
00:13:50,998 --> 00:13:56,016
It distinguishes the HEAD reflog from a
branch reflog and says which one you read

186
00:13:56,056 --> 00:14:01,199
for which incident. It states the limits
without being asked: local only, movements

187
00:14:01,239 --> 00:14:06,008
of refs only, entries that expire, and
the cases in which there is no reflog.

188
00:14:06,048 --> 00:14:11,129
And it finishes with a concrete recovery
in two steps: find the ID, then give it a

189
00:14:11,169 --> 00:14:11,441
name.

190
00:14:13,281 --> 00:14:16,208
Let's land this. You
should now be able to say:

191
00:14:16,548 --> 00:14:22,160
Work is protected by refs, then by reflogs,
then by a grace period, then by other

192
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:25,510
repositories; a recovery
moves it back to a ref.

193
00:14:25,850 --> 00:14:30,523
Only work that became an object can be
recovered from Git: committed, stashed, or

194
00:14:30,563 --> 00:14:30,930
staged.

195
00:14:31,270 --> 00:14:36,841
A reflog entry holds an old value, a new
value, who, when and which command; every

196
00:14:36,881 --> 00:14:38,688
old value is a usable name.

197
00:14:39,028 --> 00:14:44,066
The HEAD reflog shows what I did in order;
a branch reflog shows where that branch

198
00:14:44,106 --> 00:14:44,313
was.

199
00:14:44,653 --> 00:14:48,113
The reflog is local, and objects
it names count as reachable.

200
00:14:49,953 --> 00:14:54,113
Read sections 13.1 to 13.3 of Chapter 13.

201
00:14:54,453 --> 00:14:59,958
Today you got the map: four layers, one
precondition, and a log of every move a ref

202
00:14:59,998 --> 00:15:03,900
made. Read a reflog in the lab
shell before the next video.

203
00:15:03,940 --> 00:15:08,109
Next time: retention, ORIG_HEAD,
and git fsck as a search tool.

204
00:15:08,149 --> 00:15:11,583
Until then, look at the
state first and type second.

205
00:15:11,623 --> 00:15:13,186
See you in the next one.
